WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.866
A data structure is a concrete

00:00:02.951 --> 00:00:05.090
implementation of an ADT.

00:00:05.090 --> 00:00:07.314
It specifies the storage

00:00:07.399 --> 00:00:10.906
and the access mechanisms needed to carry

00:00:10.991 --> 00:00:13.985
out the operations the ADT defines.

00:00:14.071 --> 00:00:17.406
A list is implemented by an array-based

00:00:17.492 --> 00:00:20.143
list or a linked list; in Java,

00:00:20.143 --> 00:00:22.367
ArrayList and LinkedList.

00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:22.966
A set

00:00:22.966 --> 00:00:26.644
by a hash table or a balanced search tree,

00:00:26.644 --> 00:00:28.440
HashSet and TreeSet.

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:29.210
A queue

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:32.460
by a circular array or a linked list.

00:00:32.810 --> 00:00:33.732
Why several?

00:00:33.809 --> 00:00:36.653
Because it is rarely possible to make

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.959
every operation fast at once.

00:00:38.959 --> 00:00:42.417
An array-based list reads element i at once

00:00:42.494 --> 00:00:44.492
but must shift elements to

00:00:44.569 --> 00:00:46.106
insert at the front.

00:00:46.183 --> 00:00:48.796
A linked list inserts at the front

00:00:48.873 --> 00:00:50.564
by changing two links

00:00:50.641 --> 00:00:52.793
but must walk from the start

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:54.330
to reach element i.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.831
Cars are the same: steering,

00:00:56.907 --> 00:00:58.751
accelerating and braking

00:00:58.828 --> 00:01:00.441
are the ADT of a car,

00:01:00.441 --> 00:01:02.975
so you can drive almost any car

00:01:03.052 --> 00:01:05.664
even though the engines underneath

00:01:05.741 --> 00:01:07.200
are very different.

00:01:07.780 --> 00:01:10.947
An algorithm is a step-by-step procedure

00:01:11.026 --> 00:01:13.718
that uses ADTs and data structures

00:01:13.797 --> 00:01:15.064
to solve a task.

00:01:15.064 --> 00:01:18.627
It is specified by its input and its output.

00:01:18.627 --> 00:01:19.656
For sorting,

00:01:19.656 --> 00:01:22.664
the input is a sequence of n numbers,

00:01:22.744 --> 00:01:25.198
and the output is a permutation

00:01:25.277 --> 00:01:27.415
of the input, a reordering

00:01:27.415 --> 00:01:30.740
in which each number is at most the next.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:34.307
Like an ADT, the specification says what

00:01:34.388 --> 00:01:37.846
a correct output is, not how to compute it,

00:01:37.846 --> 00:01:39.696
and it has two halves.

00:01:39.696 --> 00:01:41.787
The sorted output passes.

00:01:41.787 --> 00:01:43.718
One to six is in order,

00:01:43.718 --> 00:01:47.096
but it is not a permutation of the input:

00:01:47.096 --> 00:01:47.659
false.

00:01:47.659 --> 00:01:51.117
And the last output has the right numbers

00:01:51.117 --> 00:01:53.450
but not in order: false too.

00:01:54.030 --> 00:01:56.249
Put the three ideas together

00:01:56.328 --> 00:01:57.834
and you get layers.

00:01:57.834 --> 00:02:00.845
Take breadth-first search on a graph.

00:02:00.845 --> 00:02:02.667
from lecture 19.

00:02:02.667 --> 00:02:05.837
The problem: visit the vertices nearest

00:02:05.916 --> 00:02:07.422
to the start first.

00:02:07.422 --> 00:02:09.006
The algorithm, BFS

00:02:09.006 --> 00:02:11.542
uses only the queue operations

00:02:11.542 --> 00:02:13.206
enqueue and dequeue.

00:02:13.206 --> 00:02:14.870
The queue is an ADT

00:02:14.950 --> 00:02:17.406
implemented by a circular array

00:02:17.485 --> 00:02:19.070
or by a linked list.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:23.068
Because BFS calls only the queue operations

00:02:23.068 --> 00:02:26.551
switching the implementation is one word.

00:02:26.551 --> 00:02:30.283
Both runs visit 0, 1, 2, 3, 4

00:02:30.283 --> 00:02:30.783
5.

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:33.235
The best implementation depends

00:02:33.303 --> 00:02:35.680
on the requirements: memory limits

00:02:35.680 --> 00:02:37.175
the size of the data

00:02:37.175 --> 00:02:39.620
and which operations are used most.

00:02:39.688 --> 00:02:41.997
And you can change your mind later

00:02:42.065 --> 00:02:44.170
without touching the algorithm.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:45.715
One warning:

00:02:45.715 --> 00:02:48.529
an ADT promises only what it says

00:02:48.529 --> 00:02:51.905
and the set ADT says nothing about order.

00:02:51.986 --> 00:02:54.076
A TreeSet happens to keep

00:02:54.157 --> 00:02:57.453
the elements sorted; a HashSet does not.

00:02:57.533 --> 00:03:00.669
Code that relies on the order of a HashSet

00:03:00.749 --> 00:03:03.322
relies on the physical form

00:03:03.322 --> 00:03:05.653
and may break when the data,

00:03:05.653 --> 00:03:09.030
the capacity or the Java version changes.

00:03:09.610 --> 00:03:11.956
To see one ADT with two very

00:03:12.039 --> 00:03:14.134
different physical forms,

00:03:14.134 --> 00:03:17.820
here is the integer list implemented twice

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:19.076
with an array

00:03:19.076 --> 00:03:21.924
and with a chain of linked nodes.

00:03:21.924 --> 00:03:24.270
Each class counts its work:

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.621
shifts for elements moved in the array,

00:03:27.621 --> 00:03:31.307
hops for links followed in the linked list.

00:03:31.307 --> 00:03:34.490
First, insert 10, 20 and 30.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:37.118
Now insert 5 at the front.

00:03:37.197 --> 00:03:40.260
The array must shift all three elements

00:03:40.339 --> 00:03:42.145
one place to the right.

00:03:42.145 --> 00:03:45.680
The linked list just makes a new first node.

00:03:46.030 --> 00:03:48.755
Insert 15 at position 2.

00:03:48.755 --> 00:03:51.919
The array shifts 20 and 30.

00:03:51.919 --> 00:03:54.908
The linked list follows one link

00:03:54.908 --> 00:03:57.897
to reach the node before the gap

00:03:57.897 --> 00:04:00.270
and links in the new node.

00:04:00.620 --> 00:04:02.537
Get position 4.

00:04:02.537 --> 00:04:05.307
The array reads one cell.

00:04:05.307 --> 00:04:09.248
The linked list walks from the head

00:04:09.248 --> 00:04:10.420
four hops.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:12.458
Remove position 0.

00:04:12.458 --> 00:04:14.547
The array closes the gap

00:04:14.547 --> 00:04:16.476
shifting 4 elements.

00:04:16.476 --> 00:04:19.450
The linked list just moves its head.

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:21.453
Same list, same answers.

00:04:21.522 --> 00:04:23.589
But the array made 9 shifts

00:04:23.658 --> 00:04:25.656
and the linked list 6 hops

00:04:25.656 --> 00:04:28.067
the work went to different places.

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:30.478
Neither structure wins everywhere

00:04:30.478 --> 00:04:32.200
which is why both exist.

00:04:32.780 --> 00:04:33.377
Finally,

00:04:33.451 --> 00:04:36.584
a problem that shows why a working program

00:04:36.659 --> 00:04:38.598
is not yet a good program.

00:04:38.598 --> 00:04:41.582
Given N numbers, find the k-th largest.

00:04:41.582 --> 00:04:44.714
For these 10 numbers and k equals 3,

00:04:44.714 --> 00:04:46.654
the answer is 31.

00:04:46.654 --> 00:04:49.712
The largest is 40, then 33,

00:04:49.712 --> 00:04:50.980
then 31.

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:52.417
Solution 1:

00:04:52.417 --> 00:04:55.094
Read the numbers into an array,

00:04:55.094 --> 00:04:57.520
sort it in descending order,

00:04:57.520 --> 00:05:00.698
and return the element in position k.

00:05:00.698 --> 00:05:01.869
Solution 2:

00:05:01.869 --> 00:05:04.546
Keep only the k largest so far.

00:05:04.546 --> 00:05:07.975
Read the first k elements and sort them.

00:05:07.975 --> 00:05:10.568
Then read the rest one by one.

00:05:10.652 --> 00:05:12.660
If an element is at most

00:05:12.743 --> 00:05:16.424
the k-th of the array, ignore it; otherwise,

00:05:16.424 --> 00:05:18.933
place it in its correct spot,

00:05:18.933 --> 00:05:21.442
bumping the last element out.

00:05:21.442 --> 00:05:22.446
At the end,

00:05:22.446 --> 00:05:25.290
return the element in position k.

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:27.995
Let's trace Solution 2

00:05:28.084 --> 00:05:29.855
with k equals 3.

00:05:29.855 --> 00:05:32.069
The first three: 12,

00:05:32.069 --> 00:05:33.928
5, and 31,

00:05:33.928 --> 00:05:37.470
sorted in descending order: 31,

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:38.710
12, 5.

00:05:39.060 --> 00:05:42.810
Read 8: it beats 5, so it is placed,

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:44.903
and 5 is bumped out.

00:05:44.903 --> 00:05:48.653
19: placed, and 8 is bumped out.

00:05:48.653 --> 00:05:50.833
40 goes to the front,

00:05:50.833 --> 00:05:53.100
and 12 is bumped out.

00:05:53.450 --> 00:05:56.364
2 is at most 19: ignored.

00:05:56.364 --> 00:05:58.306
27: placed,

00:05:58.306 --> 00:06:00.778
and 19 is bumped out.

00:06:00.778 --> 00:06:04.133
19 again: at most 27,

00:06:04.133 --> 00:06:05.722
so it is ignored.

00:06:05.722 --> 00:06:07.665
33: placed,

00:06:07.665 --> 00:06:10.490
and 27 is bumped out.

00:06:10.840 --> 00:06:13.965
The array now holds the three largest

00:06:13.965 --> 00:06:16.925
40, 33 and 31

00:06:16.925 --> 00:06:20.625
and its last cell is the answer: 31.

00:06:20.625 --> 00:06:24.160
Solution one, with a library sort, agrees.

00:06:24.740 --> 00:06:25.638
Now you try.

00:06:25.638 --> 00:06:28.554
With the same data and k = 3

00:06:28.629 --> 00:06:32.070
what would solution two do if the last element

00:06:32.144 --> 00:06:34.837
were 30 instead of 33?

00:06:34.837 --> 00:06:37.006
And if it were 27?

00:06:37.006 --> 00:06:39.100
Pause the video and decide.

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:44.733
30 beats 27

00:06:44.733 --> 00:06:47.891
the third element of 40, 31

00:06:47.891 --> 00:06:48.997
27.

00:06:49.076 --> 00:06:51.760
So it is placed between 31

00:06:51.839 --> 00:06:53.182
and 27

00:06:53.182 --> 00:06:55.392
27 is bumped out

00:06:55.392 --> 00:06:57.840
and the answer becomes 30.

00:06:58.190 --> 00:07:01.241
27 is equal to the third element

00:07:01.241 --> 00:07:02.519
so it is ignored

00:07:02.519 --> 00:07:05.002
and the answer stays 27.

00:07:05.002 --> 00:07:07.344
Ignoring equal elements is safe

00:07:07.415 --> 00:07:09.118
because the k-th largest

00:07:09.189 --> 00:07:10.750
value does not change.

00:07:11.330 --> 00:07:13.299
Which solution is better?

00:07:13.299 --> 00:07:15.740
We can measure, by simulation

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:19.283
or count the steps, by theoretical analysis

00:07:19.362 --> 00:07:21.724
which predicts what happens on

00:07:21.803 --> 00:07:23.693
inputs too large to try.

00:07:23.693 --> 00:07:26.370
Let us count element comparisons.

00:07:26.720 --> 00:07:28.892
Solution one with bubble

00:07:28.982 --> 00:07:32.692
sort makes N - 1 + N - 2

00:07:32.692 --> 00:07:34.954
down to 1 comparisons

00:07:34.954 --> 00:07:37.759
N * (N - 1) / 2

00:07:37.759 --> 00:07:40.383
the arithmetic series again.

00:07:40.383 --> 00:07:43.369
Solution two, in the worst case

00:07:43.369 --> 00:07:46.265
makes about k^2 / 2

00:07:46.265 --> 00:07:48.437
+ (N - k) * k.

00:07:48.437 --> 00:07:50.880
For k = N / 2

00:07:50.880 --> 00:07:54.680
that is about 3/8 of N^2.

00:07:55.030 --> 00:07:57.083
Both grow like N^2

00:07:57.083 --> 00:07:59.956
so doubling N quadruples the work.

00:07:59.956 --> 00:08:02.255
The simulation confirms it

00:08:02.255 --> 00:08:05.786
for N = 1000, 2000

00:08:05.786 --> 00:08:08.660
4000, and 8000

00:08:08.660 --> 00:08:12.190
each column grows by about four each time.

00:08:12.540 --> 00:08:14.270
For the slides' example

00:08:14.342 --> 00:08:16.505
a million numbers with k =

00:08:16.577 --> 00:08:17.875
to half a million

00:08:17.947 --> 00:08:20.398
bubble sort makes about

00:08:20.470 --> 00:08:22.633
5 × 10^11 comparisons,

00:08:22.705 --> 00:08:25.805
and solution two about

00:08:25.877 --> 00:08:28.256
1.5 × 10^11 on random data.

00:08:28.256 --> 00:08:31.501
At around a billion simple steps per second

00:08:31.501 --> 00:08:32.726
that is minutes

00:08:32.798 --> 00:08:35.249
on the slower machines the example

00:08:35.322 --> 00:08:37.340
was first written for, days.

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:40.072
So writing a working program

00:08:40.149 --> 00:08:41.609
is not good enough.

00:08:41.609 --> 00:08:43.761
Both solutions are correct

00:08:43.761 --> 00:08:46.834
and both are too slow for large inputs.

00:08:46.911 --> 00:08:50.677
We want a correct algorithm that uses the minimum

00:08:50.754 --> 00:08:53.520
memory and the minimum running time.

00:08:53.870 --> 00:08:56.377
And there are better algorithms.

00:08:56.377 --> 00:08:59.118
Solution one with an N log N sort

00:08:59.118 --> 00:09:02.486
like merge sort or Java's Arrays.sort

00:09:02.565 --> 00:09:05.541
makes about 2 * 10^7

00:09:05.620 --> 00:09:08.283
comparisons for a million numbers

00:09:08.283 --> 00:09:10.163
a fraction of a second.

00:09:10.241 --> 00:09:13.217
A min-heap of the k largest makes each

00:09:13.296 --> 00:09:15.567
bump cost log k instead of k.

00:09:15.567 --> 00:09:18.935
And quickselect partitions around a pivot

00:09:18.935 --> 00:09:20.424
as quicksort does

00:09:20.424 --> 00:09:23.870
and continues on one side only: on average

00:09:23.870 --> 00:09:25.750
time proportional to N.

00:09:26.100 --> 00:09:27.890
The right algorithm turns

00:09:27.962 --> 00:09:29.608
days into milliseconds

00:09:29.680 --> 00:09:32.329
which no amount of tuning the code of

00:09:32.401 --> 00:09:34.620
solutions one and two could do.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:35.978
To sum up.

00:09:35.978 --> 00:09:39.169
One: a data structure implements an ADT

00:09:39.169 --> 00:09:42.515
and it decides the cost of each operation

00:09:42.515 --> 00:09:45.239
like nine shifts against six hops.

00:09:45.239 --> 00:09:47.651
Two: an algorithm is specified

00:09:47.729 --> 00:09:49.597
by its input and output

00:09:49.597 --> 00:09:51.931
and BFS works with any queue.

00:09:51.931 --> 00:09:52.476
Three

00:09:52.554 --> 00:09:55.511
both simple solutions to the selection

00:09:55.589 --> 00:09:58.312
problem take about N squared steps

00:09:58.312 --> 00:10:00.880
so a better algorithm is needed.

00:10:01.230 --> 00:10:03.455
Try this: write quickselect.

00:10:03.535 --> 00:10:06.475
Partition the array around a pivot so

00:10:06.554 --> 00:10:09.097
that larger elements come first

00:10:09.177 --> 00:10:11.958
then continue only in the part that

00:10:12.037 --> 00:10:14.421
contains position k minus one

00:10:14.501 --> 00:10:17.044
and check it against the sorting

00:10:17.123 --> 00:10:19.030
solution on random data.

00:10:19.380 --> 00:10:23.060
Next time, in lecture two: Java basics.

